const body = `<h1>7.1b — ArrayList Methods: Add, Remove, and Find</h1>

<div class="activity basic-app">
    <div class="activity-header">
        <span class="activity-label">Basic Application</span>
        <span class="activity-time">~20 min</span>
    </div>

    <div class="section">
        <h3>Build: Spellbook Manager</h3>
        <p>Yesterday you saw how ArrayList grows and shrinks automatically. Today you'll use the key methods — add, remove, get, set, contains — to manage a wizard's spellbook.</p>
        <p>Create a class called <code>Spellbook</code> with an <code>ArrayList&lt;String&gt; spells</code>:</p>
        <ol>
            <li>Add starting spells: "Fireball", "Heal", "Shield".</li>
            <li>Add "Lightning Bolt" at index 1 (shifting existing spells).</li>
            <li>Remove "Shield" using <code>remove()</code>.</li>
            <li>Use <code>contains()</code> to check if "Fireball" is still there.</li>
            <li>Use <code>set()</code> to upgrade "Fireball" to "Inferno".</li>
            <li>Get the spell at index 2. Print it.</li>
            <li>Print <code>spells.size()</code> after each operation.</li>
        </ol>
        <p><strong>One flag before you start:</strong> <code>contains()</code> is not on
        the exam sheet. Use it here while you build, and know the version you can write
        on exam day: a loop over <code>spells</code> that compares each element with
        <code>equals</code> and returns true when one matches. The
        <a href="../exam/java-quick-reference.html">Java Quick Reference</a> has the full
        trade table.</p>
    </div>

    <div class="section">
        <h3>Observe Index Shifts</h3>
        <p>After removing an element from the middle, what happens to the indexes of elements that were after it? After inserting at index 1, what happens to the element that was at index 1?</p>
    </div>
</div>

<div class="resources">
    <h3>Reference Docs</h3>
    <ul>
        <li><a href="../docs/unit-07/arraylist-basics.html">ArrayList</a></li>
    </ul>
</div>`;
